<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sol Invictus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Invincible Sun, Unconquered Sun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sol Invictus, the &quot;Invincible&quot; or &quot;Unconquered Sun&quot;, was the official sun god of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. The emperor Aurelian revived his cult in 274 CE, promoted him as the chief god of the empire and dedicated a new temple to him on 25 December 274; according to some scholars he also instituted on that day, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a> of the Roman calendar, the festival Dies Natalis Solis Invicti. Scholars are divided on whether Sol Invictus was a Syrian sun god distinct from the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> god Sol or belonged to a single, continuous Roman cult of Sol. He often appeared on imperial coinage, and on that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> until 325/326. The last known inscription referring to him dates to 387 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sol-invictus.jpg" alt="Sol Invictus" width="330" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist Unknown artist · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Disc_Sol_BM_GR1899.12-1.2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…their traditional gods, the emperor’s genius and local deities, and traders and legions brought home cults from Egypt, Greece, Iberia, India and Persia, among them those of Cybele, Isis, Mithras and <b>Sol Invictus</b>; Aurelian (270–275) established an official cult of the Palmyrene Sol Invictus in Rome’s Campus Martius.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>On 25 December 274 the emperor Aurelian dedicated a temple in Rome to <b>Sol Invictus</b>, the &quot;Unconquered Sun&quot;, fulfilling a vow made after his campaign against Palmyra and paying for it with the city's spoils.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sol Invictus</b>, the &quot;Unconquered Sun&quot;, was the official sun god of the late Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>The emperor Aurelian dedicated the Temple of the Sun in Rome to <b>Sol Invictus</b>, fulfilling a vow made after his campaign against Palmyra and paid for with its spoils.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-25">25 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The day honoured <b>Sol Invictus</b>, the official sun god of the late empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-7">7 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
